<spell-notebook "button usage">
	<header level="1">
		Button
		<header>A button indicates a possible user action.</>
	</>
	<button-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#state-texts">State texts</>
			<item link="#invoker-commands">Invoker commands</>
			<item link="#forms">Forms</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Write `<ui-button>` with its text inside, and add attributes for the look.

			Most attributes become Fomantic's class words, on the real button in the shadow root:
			`<ui-button size="small" color="primary" appearance="basic">` draws `ui small basic primary button`.

			- A boolean attribute is true when it's there, or empty.
			  `"true"` and `"yes"` mean true too;  `"false"` and `"no"` mean false.
			- `medium` is a real size meaning "default":  it draws no class.
			- Three attributes stand in for slots, and children in the slot win:
			  - `icon` for the `icon` slot
			  - `label` for the default slot (`content` is its old name)
			  - `badge` for the `label` slot:  the joined badge
			- `link` draws an `<a>` in place of the button, so it navigates like a link.
			- Wrap buttons in `<ui-buttons>` to join them.
			  The group's words (`size`, `color` ...) reach every button in it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			A plain click is the native `click`.
			A toggle button also sends `ui-selected` as it turns on, and `ui-deselected` as it turns off.
			Both carry the new state in `event.detail.active`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const toggle = document.querySelector("ui-button[toggle]")
			toggle.addEventListener("ui-selected", () => console.log("on"))
			toggle.addEventListener("ui-deselected", () => console.log("off"))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="State texts">
		<markdown>
			Fomantic's `state` behaviour is two attributes on a toggle button here, not a module of its own:

			- `inactive-text` replaces the content while the button is off.
			- `active-text` replaces it while the button is on (`selected`).
			- With only one of them, the button's own content shows in the other state.

			They're live on the Examples tab, under [State texts](/edit/@spell/notebooks/Button/Examples.spellx#states-state-texts).

			The label SAYS the state, so a toggle button with a state text leaves `aria-pressed` off.
			A toggle button whose name changes would announce the state twice (WAI-ARIA APG).
			Listen for `ui-selected` and `ui-deselected` as usual.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const follow = document.querySelector("ui-button[active-text]")
			follow.addEventListener("ui-selected", () => startFollowing())
			follow.addEventListener("ui-deselected", () => stopFollowing())
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Invoker commands">
		<markdown>
			A `<ui-button>` drives other elements with no script, as a native button does:

			- `commandfor` is the target's id, looked up in the button's own tree.
			- `command` is the action.

			The actions:

			- A `<dialog>` and a popover take the browser's own commands.
			- A custom command's name starts with two dashes.
			  It reaches the target as a `command` event.
			- `<ui-modal>` answers three custom commands.

			```text
			<dialog>
			  show-modal, close, request-close
			a popover
			  show-popover, hide-popover,
			  toggle-popover
			<ui-modal>
			  --show, --close, --toggle
			```

			The button inside the shadow root can't see a light-DOM id,
			so the element hands it the target itself (`commandForElement`).
			In a browser without invoker commands (`UI.browser.supports.invokers`),
			a click runs the built-in commands itself.
		</>

		<section label="Popup and modal" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example "Popup and modal" description="A button opens a popup and a modal with `commandfor` and `command`, no script.">
				<button commandfor="button-invoker-popup" command="toggle-popover">Toggle popup</button>
				<popup id="button-invoker-popup" open-on="manual" label="Toggled by the button, no script."/>
				<button commandfor="button-invoker-modal" command="--show" color="primary">Open modal</button>
				<modal id="button-invoker-modal" heading="Invoked" content="Opened by a ui-button." closable/>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Forms">
		<markdown>
			A `<ui-button>` is a form-associated element, so it works inside a `<form>` like a native button.

			- `type` is `button` by default, so a plain one never submits by accident.
			- `type="submit"` submits the form round it with `form.requestSubmit()`,
			  so validation and the submit event run.
			  `type="reset"` resets it.
			- `name` / `value` are submitted with the form when this button submits it.
			- `button.click()` from a script presses it as a click would:  it submits, toggles or follows its link.
			  So Enter in a `<ui-input>` submits through the form's submit button.
			- A disabled `<fieldset>` disables the buttons inside it.
			- To submit, the buttons need a real `<form>` in the light DOM:  inside a `<ui-form>` too.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-form>
			  <form action="/subscribe">
			    <ui-input name="email" type="email" required></ui-input>
			    <ui-button type="submit" name="action" value="subscribe" color="primary">Subscribe</ui-button>
			    <ui-button type="reset" appearance="basic">Reset</ui-button>
			  </form>
			</ui-form>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			The shadow root holds a real `<button>` (or `<a>`), so the browser does the keyboard work.

			- **Tab** / **Shift+Tab** moves focus to and from the button. The host delegates focus
			  (`delegatesFocus`), so it is never a tab stop of its own and the focus ring is drawn on the inner
			  control.
			- **Enter** or **Space** clicks a button; **Enter** follows a `link`.
			- A toggle button flips `selected` on **Enter** or **Space**, as a click does.
			- A `disabled` button is skipped by **Tab**.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Focus, **Enter** and **Space**, form submission and the accessible name
			  all come from the browser's own `<button>`;  with `link`, from its `<a>`.
			- An icon-only button needs a name:  put `aria-label` on the element.
			  It forwards it to the inner button, and shows it as the button's tooltip.
			- A toggle button sets `aria-pressed`, and keeps it in step with `selected`.
			  Not with a state text:  see [State texts](#state-texts).
			- `loading` sets `aria-busy`.
			  `disabled` disables the inner button.
			- `<ui-buttons>` has the group role:  give it an `aria-label` when the group needs a name.
			- If the element fails to render, its native fallback keeps a plain `<button>` styled by the same
			  class words, so the button still renders, focuses and submits its form.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>
			Define the elements first (load the bundle, or `<ui-root>`), then use the tag like any other. Events
			are `ui-*`, so use your framework's custom-event syntax.
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-button toggle onui-selected={() => setOn(true)} onui-deselected={() => setOn(false)} color="primary">Vote</ui-button>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-button toggle @ui-selected="on = true" @ui-deselected="on = false" color="primary">Vote</ui-button>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-button toggle on-ui-selected={(event) => setOn(event.detail.active)} on-ui-deselected={(event) => setOn(event.detail.active)} color="primary">
			  Vote
			</ui-button>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
